course: dictionary
lesson: 12110
title: Cole, the cabbage word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words hide inside other words, and the start of a word can be a clue. «Mum made a bowl of coleslaw for the picnic.» You may not know cole, but slaw is a salad, and cole is the first part of coleslaw, so the word is about a salad.
explain: Cole is an old word for cabbage and for plants in the cabbage family, such as kale. «The old farm book described cole crops growing in the fields.» Cole crops are plants from the cabbage family that farmers grow. Try your meaning in the sentence: cabbage fits, so you are right.
explain: Cole is also a name. «Our new teacher is Mrs Cole.» The capital letters and the word Mrs tell you that Cole is a surname, a family name, not a plant. Always ask what the sentence is about: food, or a person?
fact: Cole is an old word for cabbage and for plants in the cabbage family, such as kale.
fact: Coleslaw is a salad of shredded raw cabbage and carrot with mayonnaise.
fact: Cole is an uncountable noun, so you do not usually say one cole in the way you say one apple.
fact: Cole is also a first name and a surname, so a capital letter can show it is a person's name.
fact: Kale and cauliflower share the same root as cole, so they belong to the same plant family.
exercise: «Mum made a bowl of coleslaw for the picnic.» What is coleslaw? | A drink made from apples | A cake with cream | *A salad of shredded cabbage and carrot with mayonnaise | A hot soup made with potatoes
exercise: «The old farm book described cole crops growing in the fields.» What does cole mean here? | A tractor | A type of hat | A farm animal | *Cabbage and plants in the cabbage family
exercise: «Our new teacher is Mrs Cole.» Is Cole a plant or a person's name? | A food | A town | *A person's name | A plant
test: Which word is the first part of «coleslaw»? | cold | *cole | slow | cool
test: Which plant is in the cabbage family? | grass | rose | carrot | *kale
exam: Dad says, «Pass the cole, please.» What is cole in this sentence? | A type of cake | *A cabbage-family plant | A teacher's name | A river
